Title: A CHRISTMAS CAROL
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from the book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
Marley was dead, to begin with. Old Marley was as dead as a door-nail. This must be distinctly understood, or nothing wonderful can come of the story I am going to relate.

FADE IN:

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

Fog presses against the window. Inside, it is cold, bleak, biting weather. 

EBENEZER SCROOGE, a squeezing, wrenching, grasping, scraping, clutching, covetous old sinner, sits at his desk. His pointed nose is nipped by the cold, his eyes red, his thin lips blue.

In a dismal little cell beyond, a sort of tank, his clerk BOB CRATCHIT copies letters. Bob's fire is so small it looks like one coal. Bob puts on his white comforter and tries to warm himself at the candle.

The door flies open. FRED, Scrooge's nephew, enters. He is all in a glow, his face ruddy and handsome, his breath smoking in the cold room.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? What reason have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
Come, then. What right have you to be dismal? What reason have you to be morose? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug.

FRED
Don't be cross, uncle!

SCROOGE
What else can I be, when I live in such a world of fools as this? If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with 'Merry Christmas' on his lips, should be boiled with his own pudding, and buried with a stake of holly through his heart. He should!

FRED
Uncle!

SCROOGE
Nephew! Keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

FRED
But you don't keep it.

SCROOGE
Let me leave it alone, then. Much good may it do you! Much good it has ever done you!

FRED
I have always thought of Christmas time as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time. And therefore, uncle, though it has never put a scrap of gold or silver in my pocket, I believe that it has done me good, and will do me good; and I say, God bless it!

Bob Cratchit involuntarily applauds from the Tank. Realizing his mistake, he pokes his tiny fire.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation!

FRED
Don't be angry, uncle. Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I want nothing from you; I ask nothing of you; why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
A Merry Christmas, uncle!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred leaves the room without an angry word. He stops to exchange cordial greetings with Bob Cratchit.

As Fred exits, MR. HOWELL, a portly gentleman, enters, holding books and papers. He bows to Scrooge.

MR. HOWELL
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe. Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge, or Mr. Marley?

SCROOGE
Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years. He died seven years ago, this very night.

MR. HOWELL
At this festive season of the year, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute. Hundreds of thousands are in want of common comforts, sir.

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

MR. HOWELL
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

MR. HOWELL
They are. Still, I wish I could say they were not.

SCROOGE
The Treadmill and the Poor Law are in full vigour, then?

MR. HOWELL
Both very busy, sir. A few of us are endeavouring to raise a fund to buy the Poor some meat and drink, and means of warmth. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

MR. HOWELL
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. I don't make merry myself at Christmas and I can't afford to make idle people merry. I help to support the establishments I have mentioned--they cost enough; and those who are badly off must go there.

MR. HOWELL
Many can't go there; and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

Mr. Howell withdraws. 

Scrooge dismounts his stool. Bob Cratchit instantly snuffs his candle out and puts on his hat.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. If I was to stop half-a-crown for it, you'd think yourself ill-used, I'll be bound?

Bob smiles faintly.

SCROOGE
And yet, you don't think me ill-used, when I pay a day's wages for no work.

BOB CRATCHIT
It is only once a year, sir.

SCROOGE
A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December! But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Bob promises he will, and runs out into the cold.

EXT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

A gloomy suite of rooms in a lowering pile of building up a dark yard. Fog and frost hang about the black old gateway.

Scrooge approaches his door. He puts his key in the lock.

He looks at the large door knocker. Without undergoing any intermediate process of change, the knocker becomes a face.

MARLEY'S FACE. It has a dismal light about it, like a bad lobster in a dark cellar. Ghostly spectacles are turned up on its ghostly forehead.

Scrooge stares. It is a knocker again.

He turns the key sturdily, walks in, and lights his candle.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

Scrooge double-locks his door. He puts on his dressing-gown, slippers, and nightcap, and sits before a very low fire to eat his gruel.

Suddenly, a disused bell hanging in the room begins to swing. It rings out loudly. Every bell in the house joins in.

The bells cease. A clanking noise comes from deep down below, as if someone were dragging a heavy chain over casks in the cellar. The noise comes up the stairs. It comes straight towards his door.

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

The heavy door swings open. The dying flame leaps up.

MARLEY'S GHOST enters. It is Jacob Marley, transparent, bound in a long chain made of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds, and heavy purses wrought in steel.

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY'S GHOST
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley. You don't believe in me.

SCROOGE
I don't. You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

The Spirit raises a frightful cry and shakes its chain with an appalling noise. It takes off the bandage round its head, and its lower jaw drops down upon its breast.

Scrooge falls upon his knees and clasps his hands before his face.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen. If that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death!

SCROOGE
You are fettered. Tell me why?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard. I girded it on of my own free will. Would you know the weight and length of the strong coil you bear yourself? It was full as heavy and as long as this, seven Christmas Eves ago. You have laboured on it, since. It is a ponderous chain!

SCROOGE
Jacob. Old Jacob Marley, tell me more. Speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY'S GHOST
I have none to give. I am here to-night to warn you, that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
I--I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread. Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One.

The Ghost binds its wrapper round its head, walks backward to the window, and floats out upon the bleak, dark night.

Scrooge collapses into bed and falls asleep instantly.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT (LATER)

The clock tolls a deep, dull, hollow ONE.

Light flashes up in the room. The curtains of Scrooge's bed are drawn aside by a hand.

Scrooge finds himself face to face with the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST. It is a strange figure--like a child, yet like an old man. It wears a tunic of purest white, holding a branch of fresh green holly. From the crown of its head springs a bright clear jet of light.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am!

SCROOGE
Who, and what are you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past. Rise! and walk with me!

The Spirit clasps Scrooge's arm. They pass through the wall.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT (PAST)

The warehouse is snug, warm, and bright. A fiddler tunes up.

OLD FEZZIWIG, in a Welsh wig, sits behind a high desk. He rubs his hands and laughs all over himself.

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick!

A young man comes briskly in. It is Scrooge's former self, with his fellow-apprentice.

SCROOGE
Why, it's old Fezziwig! Bless his heart; it's Fezziwig alive again! And Dick Wilkins, to be sure!

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, my boys! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer! Let's have the shutters up before a man can say Jack Robinson!

The young men clear the floor. In comes Mrs. Fezziwig, the Miss Fezziwigs, the housemaid, the baker, the cook. The fiddler strikes up. They dance wildly, joyously.

The Ghost of Christmas Past turns to Scrooge.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter, to make these silly folks so full of gratitude. He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money. Is that so much that he deserves this praise?

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome. The happiness he gives, is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

Scrooge stops, feeling the Spirit's glance.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
What is the matter?

SCROOGE
Nothing. I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

EXT. CITY STREET - DAY (PAST)

Scrooge sees himself, older now, his face showing the signs of care and avarice. He sits beside a fair young girl in a mourning-dress, BELLE. Tears are in her eyes.

BELLE
It matters little. To you, very little. Another idol has displaced me; and if it can cheer and comfort you in time to come, as I would have tried to do, I have no just cause to grieve.

YOUNG SCROOGE
What Idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one. You fear the world too much. I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you.

YOUNG SCROOGE
Even if I have grown so much wiser, what then? I am not changed towards you.

BELLE
Our contract is an old one. It was made when we were both poor and content to be so. You are changed. That which promised happiness when we were one in heart, is fraught with misery now that we are two. I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

She leaves him.

SCROOGE
Spirit! show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
One shadow more!

SCROOGE
No more! I don't wish to see it!

Scrooge seizes the Spirit's extinguisher-cap and presses it down upon its head with all his force. The light streams from under it. Scrooge reels to bed and sinks into a heavy sleep.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

Scrooge awakens. A blaze of ruddy light streams upon his bed. He shuffles to the adjoining room.

The room is transformed into a perfect grove of living green, gleaming with berries. Heaped upon the floor is a throne of turkeys, geese, game, plum-puddings, and bowls of punch.

Sitting in easy state is a jolly Giant, the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT, wearing a simple green robe bordered with white fur, and a holly wreath upon its head.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in! and know me better, man! I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me!

SCROOGE
Spirit, conduct me where you will. I went forth last night on compulsion, and I learnt a lesson which is working now. To-night, if you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - DAY (PRESENT)

A poor, four-roomed house. MRS. CRATCHIT, brave in cheap ribbons, lays the cloth.

BOB CRATCHIT enters, his threadbare clothes darned up and brushed. On his shoulder sits TINY TIM, bearing a little crutch, his limbs supported by an iron frame.

The young Cratchits hustle Tiny Tim off to hear the pudding singing in the copper.

MRS. CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

BOB CRATCHIT
As good as gold, and better. He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day, who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

Bob's voice trembles. Tiny Tim returns to his stool by the fire.

The goose is brought in. It is a feathered phenomenon. Eked out by apple-sauce and mashed potatoes, it is a sufficient dinner for the whole family.

Mrs. Cratchit brings in the pudding, blazing in ignited brandy, bedight with Christmas holly.

The dinner is done. Bob serves hot stuff from a jug into two tumblers and a custard-cup without a handle.

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Tim sits very close to his father's side. Bob holds his withered little hand.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head, overcome with penitence and grief.

BOB CRATCHIT
Mr. Scrooge! I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed! I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon. Such an odious, stingy, hard, unfeeling man as Mr. Scrooge.

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear, the children! Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's, not for his. Long life to him!

INT. FRED'S HOUSE - NIGHT (PRESENT)

A bright, dry, gleaming room. Fred laughs heartily, holding his sides. His wife, CLARA, and friends roar out lustily with him.

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

CLARA
More shame for him, Fred!

FRED
He's a comical old fellow, and not so pleasant as he might be. But his offences carry their own punishment. Who suffers by his ill whims? Himself, always. He takes it into his head to dislike us, and he won't come and dine with us. What's the consequence? He don't lose much of a dinner.

CLARA
Indeed, I think he loses a very good dinner.

FRED
I mean to give him the same chance every year, whether he likes it or not, for I pity him. He may rail at Christmas till he dies, but he can't help thinking better of it if he finds me going there, in good temper, year after year, and saying Uncle Scrooge, how are you?

Fred raises his glass.

FRED
A Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year to the old man, whatever he is! Uncle Scrooge!

EXT. BLEAK MOOR - NIGHT (PRESENT)

Scrooge and the Ghost stand in an open place. The Ghost has grown older, its hair grey.

SCROOGE
Are spirits' lives so short?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
My life upon this globe, is very brief. It ends to-night at midnight.

SCROOGE
Forgive me, but I see something strange, protruding from your skirts. Is it a foot or a claw?

From the foldings of its robe, the Spirit brings two children; wretched, abject, frightful, hideous, miserable. A boy and a girl. Yellow, meagre, ragged, scowling, wolfish.

SCROOGE
Spirit! are they yours?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
They are Man's. This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, and all of their degree, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom, unless the writing be erased.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes twelve. The Ghost of Christmas Present vanishes.

A solemn Phantom, draped and hooded, comes like a mist along the ground towards Scrooge.

EXT. CITY EXCHANGE - DAY (FUTURE)

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME, shrouded in a deep black garment, points its outstretched hand.

Scrooge stands near a knot of wealthy businessmen, including MR. GRUBB and MR. BLAKE.

MR. GRUBB
No, I don't know much about it, either way. I only know he's dead.

MR. BLAKE
When did he die?

MR. GRUBB
Last night, I believe.

MR. BLAKE
What has he done with his money?

MR. GRUBB
I haven't heard. Left it to his company, perhaps. He hasn't left it to me. That's all I know. It's likely to be a very cheap funeral, for upon my life I don't know of anybody to go to it.

They stroll away, laughing. Scrooge looks to the Phantom for explanation. The Phantom glides on.

INT. OLD JOE'S SHOP - NIGHT (FUTURE)

A low-browed, beetling shop full of iron, old rags, bottles, and bones. OLD JOE sits by a charcoal stove.

MRS. DILBER, a charwoman, throws a heavy bundle on the floor.

MRS. DILBER
Every person has a right to take care of themselves. He always did. Who's the worse for the loss of a few things like these? Not a dead man, I suppose.

OLD JOE
No, indeed.

MRS. DILBER
If he wanted to keep 'em after he was dead, a wicked old screw, why wasn't he natural in his lifetime? If he had been, he'd have had somebody to look after him when he was struck with Death, instead of lying gasping out his last there, alone by himself.

She drags out a large and heavy roll of dark stuff.

OLD JOE
What do you call this? Bed-curtains! You don't mean to say you took 'em down, rings and all, with him lying there?

MRS. DILBER
Yes I do. Why not? He isn't likely to take cold without 'em.

Scrooge recoils in horror.

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way, now.

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - DAY (FUTURE)

Quiet. Very quiet. Mrs. Cratchit and the girls are sewing.

MRS. CRATCHIT
It makes my eyes weak by candle-light; and I wouldn't show weak eyes to your father when he comes home, for the world. It must be near his time.

PETER
Past it rather. But I think he has walked a little slower than he used, these few last evenings, mother.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I have known him walk with Tiny Tim upon his shoulder, very fast indeed. But he was very light to carry, and his father loved him so, that it was no trouble.

Bob Cratchit comes in. He looks at the work upon the table.

BOB CRATCHIT
I promised him that I would walk there on a Sunday. My little, little child! My little child!

He breaks down all at once.

SCROOGE
Spectre, something informs me that our parting moment is at hand. Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead?

EXT. CHURCHYARD - DAY (FUTURE)

Walled in by houses, overrun by grass and weeds. A worthy place.

The Spirit stands among the graves, and points down to One.

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone to which you point, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

The Spirit points downward to the grave.

Scrooge creeps towards it, trembling. He reads upon the stone of the neglected grave his own name: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

The finger points from the grave to him, and back again.

SCROOGE
No, Spirit! Oh no, no! Hear me! I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse. Why show me this, if I am past all hope!

Scrooge falls upon the ground, clutching at its robe.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. I will not shut out the lessons that they teach. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

He catches the spectral hand. The Phantom shrinks, collapses, and dwindles down into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - DAY

The bedpost is his own. The bed is his own, the room is his own.

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven, and the Christmas Time be praised for this!

He scrambles out of bed, laughing and crying in the same breath.

SCROOGE
I don't know what to do! I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy. I am as giddy as a drunken man. A merry Christmas to everybody! A happy New Year to all the world. Hallo here! Whoop! Hallo!

He runs to the window, opens it, and puts out his head. Golden sunlight; Heavenly sky; sweet fresh air; merry bells.

SCROOGE
What's to-day!

THOMAS, a boy in Sunday clothes, loiters below.

THOMAS
Eh?

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

THOMAS
To-day! Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it. The Spirits have done it all in one night. Hallo, my fine fellow! Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one?

THOMAS
I should hope I did.

SCROOGE
An intelligent boy! A remarkable boy! Do you know whether they've sold the prize Turkey that was hanging up there? Not the little prize Turkey: the big one?

THOMAS
What, the one as big as me? It's hanging there now.

SCROOGE
Is it? Go and buy it. Tell 'em to bring it here. Come back with the man, and I'll give you a shilling. Come back with him in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

Thomas is off like a shot.

SCROOGE
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! He sha'n't know who sends it. It's twice the size of Tiny Tim.

EXT. CITY STREET - DAY

Scrooge walks with his hands behind him, regarding everyone with a delighted smile.

He beholds Mr. Howell, the portly gentleman who had walked into his counting-house the day before. Scrooge quickens his pace and takes the old gentleman by both his hands.

SCROOGE
My dear sir. How do you do? I hope you succeeded yesterday. It was very kind of you. A merry Christmas to you, sir!

MR. HOWELL
Mr. Scrooge?

SCROOGE
Yes. That is my name, and I fear it may not be pleasant to you. Allow me to ask your pardon. And will you have the goodness--

Scrooge whispers in his ear.

MR. HOWELL
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it, I assure you. Will you do me that favour?

MR. HOWELL
My dear sir, I don't know what to say to such munifi--

SCROOGE
Don't say anything, please. Come and see me. Will you come and see me?

MR. HOWELL
I will!

INT. FRED'S HOUSE - DAY

Fred and Clara are looking at the dining table.

Scrooge turns the lock gently, and sidles his face in, round the door.

SCROOGE
Fred!

FRED
Why bless my soul! who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred nearly shakes his arm off.

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

The clock strikes nine. No Bob. A quarter past. No Bob. He is full eighteen minutes and a half behind his time.

Scrooge sits with his door wide open.

Bob Cratchit rushes into the Tank, his hat off, driving away with his pen as if trying to overtake nine o'clock.

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
You are? Yes. I think you are. Step this way, sir, if you please.

BOB CRATCHIT
It's only once a year, sir. It shall not be repeated. I was making rather merry yesterday, sir.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend, I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore...

Scrooge leaps from his stool, and gives Bob a dig in the waistcoat.

SCROOGE
And therefore I am about to raise your salary!

Bob trembles, and gets a little nearer to his ruler.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you, for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family, and we will discuss your affairs this very afternoon, over a Christmas bowl of smoking bishop, Bob! Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
Scrooge was better than his word. He did it all, and infinitely more; and to Tiny Tim, who did NOT die, he was a second father. He became as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man, as the good old city knew.

TINY TIM
(V.O.)
God bless Us, Every One!

FADE OUT.

THE END
